## Why This Is Asked
Tests must cover offline-first collaboration where convergence semantics and conflict resolution drive UX and correctness. This scenario exercises WebSocket events, presence, and merge policies across latency, partitions, and reconnections.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time sync vs offline edits
- Presence and attribution
- Conflict resolution (CRDT/operational transform)
- Test isolation and determinism

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright skeleton for offline-to-online convergence test
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');
test('offline edit converges with deterministic merge', async ({browser}) => {
  const ctxA = await browser.newContext();
  const ctxB = await browser.newContext();
  const pageA = await (await ctxA.newPage()).goto('/doc/1');
  const pageB = await (await ctxB.newPage()).goto('/doc/1');
  // enable offline, make edits, reconnect, assert merged state within 5s
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate varying network latency?
- How would you validate changing merge policies for A/B tests?

